    <section class="kidney-series" id="kidney-series">
        <header>
            <p class="eyebrow">VitalReach Kidney Health Ghana Series</p>
            <h2>
                Know your kidneys.<br>
                <em>Protect your life.</em>
            </h2>
            <p>Your kidneys work quietly every day—filtering waste, balancing fluids and electrolytes, helping regulate blood pressure, supporting red blood cell production and keeping bones strong. Kidney disease can also develop quietly, which is why prevention and early testing matter.</p>
        </header>
        <div class="episode-grid">
            <article>
                <span>EPISODE 01</span>
                <h3>Know your kidney numbers</h3>
                <p>Ask your healthcare professional about two important tests: eGFR, a blood-test estimate of how well your kidneys filter, and uACR, a urine test that checks for albumin, a protein that may leak into urine when kidneys are damaged. Results often need to be repeated and interpreted with your full health history.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span>EPISODE 02</span>
                <h3>Blood pressure &amp; diabetes</h3>
                <p>High blood pressure and diabetes are major kidney-health concerns. Know your blood pressure and, if you have diabetes, your blood-glucose goals. Take prescribed medicines as directed and discuss how often your kidney function and urine albumin should be checked.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span>EPISODE 03 · MEDICATION SAFETY</span>
                <h3>Pain medicines, alcohol &amp; energy drinks</h3>
                <p>Some over-the-counter pain medicines, including ibuprofen and naproxen, can harm the kidneys—especially when a person is dehydrated or already has kidney disease, diabetes or high blood pressure. Alcohol can increase certain medication risks, while highly caffeinated energy drinks may raise heart rate and blood pressure.</p>
                <p>Tramadol is a controlled prescription opioid medication. Never combine tramadol with alcohol; this can cause dangerous drowsiness, slowed breathing, overdose or death. Ask a physician or pharmacist before mixing any medicine with alcohol, caffeine, supplements or energy products.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span>EPISODE 04</span>
                <h3>Daily prevention &amp; care</h3>
                <p>Stay appropriately hydrated, eat balanced meals, limit excess salt and sugar, avoid overuse of pain medicines and attend regular checkups. If you already have heart or kidney disease, follow the fluid and nutrition plan given by your healthcare team.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
        <aside class="kidney-questions">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow light">Ask at your next visit</p>
                <h3>Five kidney-health questions</h3>
            </div>
            <ol>
                <li>What is my eGFR?</li>
                <li>What is my urine albumin or uACR result?</li>
                <li>What is my blood pressure?</li>
                <li>What is my HbA1c level if I have diabetes?</li>
                <li>How often should my kidneys be checked?</li>
            </ol>
        </aside>
        <p class="medical-note">This guide is for education and does not replace medical advice, diagnosis or treatment. Speak with a qualified healthcare professional about your individual needs. Reviewed against guidance from <a href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/kidney-disease/chronic-kidney-disease-ckd/prevention" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIDDK</a> and the <a href="https://www.kidney.org/kidney-topics/know-your-kidney-numbers-two-simple-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Kidney Foundation</a>.</p>
    </section>
